<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tay is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, about 190 km long, and the largest in the United Kingdom by volume of water. It flows from the western Highlands through Loch Tay and past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay">Firth of Tay</a> at Dundee. Its valley was a frontier of Roman campaigns and a heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, with Scone, the inauguration place of Scottish kings, near its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tay.jpg" alt="River Tay" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dudesleeper at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Matthew%27s_Church_and_Smeaton%27s_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Tay rises on the slopes of Ben Lui, about 40 km from the west coast town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oban">Oban</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> and Bute. In its upper catchment it carries several names, the River Cononish, the River Fillan and the River Dochart, before it flows into Loch Tay at Killin. It leaves the loch at Kenmore and runs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a>, which was its lowest bridging point in historical times, and below Perth it becomes tidal and enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay">Firth of Tay</a>, south of Dundee.</p><p>Over its 190 km from west to east it drains much of the lower Highlands, and, unusually among Scottish rivers, it has several major tributaries: the Earn, the Isla, the Tummel, the Almond and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. Its name is thought to come from a conjectured Brythonic word, Tausa, possibly meaning silent one or strong one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The highest flood recorded at Perth occurred in 1814, when the river rose 7 m above its usual level, partly because of a blockage of ice under Smeaton's Bridge. In January 1993 a flow of 2,268 cubic metres a second brought extensive flooding to the city, although hydroelectric schemes upstream held back runoff.</p><p>In the 19th century the Tay Rail Bridge was built across the firth at Dundee as part of the East Coast Main Line, designed by Sir Thomas Bouch and opened in May 1878. On 28 December 1879 it collapsed as a train passed over, and the whole train fell into the firth with the loss of 75 passengers and crew. The disaster was commemorated in a poem by William McGonagall and in another by Theodor Fontane. The replacement bridge opened on 11 June 1887. A ferry for passengers and vehicles ran between Craig Pier in Dundee and Newport-on-Tay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> until the Tay Road Bridge opened on 18 August 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is a Site of Special Scientific Interest and a Special Area of Conservation, important for salmon, otters, lampreys and freshwater pearl mussels. It is known for salmon fishing, and the largest rod-caught salmon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, 64 pounds, was caught on the Tay by Georgina Ballantine in 1922. The stretch around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a> is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s 40 national scenic areas and covers 5,708 hectares. Dundee is the largest city on the river, which has a catchment of about 5,200 square kilometres and the largest measured discharge of any river in the United Kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Tay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its lands in the valleys of the <b>Tay</b>, Tummel and Garry were pastoral and thinly peopled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-atholl">Kingdom of Atholl</a></li><li><blockquote>The Venicones appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, generally placed in Fife and perhaps Kinross and southern Angus, between the Forth and the <b>Tay</b>, with a settlement called Orrea.</blockquote><a href="/state/venicones">Venicones</a></li></ul></section>
